   1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
   2: """#432: the DC21285 "footbridge" driven by REAL GUEST INSTRUCTIONS, with no rig.
   3: 
   4: WHY THIS EXISTS ALONGSIDE regress/diff_footbridge.c.  The offline differential calls
   5: footbridge_effective_cycles() and dev_footbridge_access() directly, so it proves BEHAVIOUR
   6: but cannot prove the device is REACHABLE by a guest -- it would stay green under a change
   7: that unmapped it (a memory_device_register length, a DM_ flag, or machine_cats.c's
   8: `addr=0x42000000` line).  Until this probe, R6 was THE ONLY CODE ROUND IN THIS TREE WITH NO
   9: REACHABILITY WITNESS OF ANY KIND: no rig boots a footbridge and there is no m8online analog.
  10: This is that analog.  It goes through the real address decode and the real memory_rw
  11: plumbing, on a COLD DEBUGGER -- no boot, no disk image, no guest OS.
  12: 
  13: *** THE TRAP THIS PROBE EXISTS TO SURVIVE, and it is the whole reason for arm T. ***
  14: machine_cats.c:131 calls arm_setup_initial_translation_table(), which (cpu_arm.c:239-255)
  15: writes a section descriptor for i in 0..255 at VA (j<<28)+(i<<20) -> PA i<<20 -- i.e. the
  16: TOP NIBBLE OF THE VA IS IGNORED and only the low 256 MB of physical space is mapped
  17: anywhere.  The footbridge sits at PA 0x42000000, outside that window, so under the boot-time
  18: MMU it is UNREACHABLE: VA 0x42000000 aliases to PA 0x02000000 and a load returns 0.
  19: 
  20: That is not a hypothetical.  The first version of this probe returned 0x0 on every device
  21: row WITH ITS RAM CONTROL STILL GREEN, and 0x0 is exactly what "the device is not there"
  22: looks like -- so a survival-shaped or absence-shaped assertion would have CONFIRMED THE
  23: OPPOSITE CONCLUSION.  A RAM liveness row cannot see it, because VA 0x9000 maps to PA 0x9000
  24: identically with the MMU on or off.
  25: 
  26: So the guard is not a comment, it is arm T: THE SAME PROGRAM WITH THE THREE MMU-DISABLE
  27: INSTRUCTIONS REPLACED BY NOPS MUST NOT PRODUCE THE DEVICE'S ANSWER.  If arm T ever reads
  28: 0x1011, the discriminator is dead and every other row here is meaningless -- and it goes RED
  29: rather than silently green.  Arm L additionally reads CP15 c1 BACK after the mcr and asserts
  30: bit 0 is clear, so the precondition is MEASURED rather than inferred from the store having
  31: been issued.  Three separate statements, deliberately: the guest turned the MMU off, the two
  32: arms really differ in MMU state, and only the MMU-off arm can see the device.
  33: 
  34: VALUES, NOT SURVIVAL (rounds 79/80, and m8820x_sites_probe.py's header says the same).
  35: A row that only asks "did the host stay up" cannot tell a reachable device from an absent
  36: one -- an unmapped read survives too, it just returns 0.  So the two device rows assert
  37: values that ONLY dev_footbridge.c can produce:
  38:   * A1 reads VENDOR_ID and requires 0x1011 (dev_footbridge.c `case VENDOR_ID`, the
  39:     DC21285_VENDOR_ID assignment).
  40:     Nonzero and specific: the hand-assembled-encoding trap this project has been bitten by
  41:     yields 0 from a wrong register field, and 0 is the one answer this row rejects.
  42:   * A2 writes 0x12345678 to TIMER_1_LOAD and reads back 0x00345678.  THE VANISHING TOP BYTE
  43:     IS THE HANDLER'S OWN SIGNATURE: `case TIMER_1_LOAD`'s write branch (one case serves all four
  44:     timers through the fold) stores `idata & TIMER_MAX_VAL`
  45:     (0x00FFFFFF, dc21285reg.h:371).  RAM, or any decode that merely absorbed the store,
  46:     would return 0x12345678 unchanged.  This row therefore distinguishes "the device
  47:     handled it" from "the write landed somewhere", which A1 alone cannot.
  48: 
  49: THE HARNESS LEGS (#545), which replaced the kill bridge.  Until #545 this probe's failability
  50: control was arm K: arm L plus ONE guest read of IRQ_ENABLE_CLEAR (+0x18c), which the device
  51: answered with fatal() then exit(1).  #545 declines that read (and the four undefined-direction
  52: writes) instead of dying, so nothing a guest can do to this device ends the process any more
  53: -- except the two SDRAM reads at +0x100/+0x104 (`fbsdrampair`), which no arm here touches.  The
  54: control that "this harness notices a dead child" therefore has to come from the HARNESS, and
  55: it comes from two legs that need no product defect:
  56:   H1 STARTUP REJECTION: launch with an unknown `-E` name.  machine.c prints
  57:      `Sorry, emulation "<name>" is unknown.` and main.c exits 1 before any prompt.  The row
  58:      requires NO prompt AND that diagnostic naming THIS run's input AND `WIFEXITED && == 1`.
  59:      The text clause does all the work: five different startup refusals (bad option, bad -M,
  60:      missing file, ...) ALL exit 1 with no prompt, so "exit != 0" or "exit == 1" alone proves
  61:      nothing (measured, pass 1).
  62:   H2 QUIT AFTER A VERIFIED PROLOGUE: arm L's program, stepped to its end, a FRESH `reg` proving
  63:      r3 bit 0 clear, r5 = 0x11223344, r6 = 0x1011 and r1 = 0x00345678 (the prologue really
  64:      ran), then `quit`, then ONE MORE ordinary `reg`: it must fail and `dead` must be set
  65:      BEFORE teardown, and the reap must be `WIFEXITED && == 0`.  Both non-obvious clauses are
  66:      load-bearing: a child that died BEFORE the prologue finished produces `next send fails`
  67:      and `dead` IDENTICALLY, and is separated only by exit == 0 and the fresh-reg values --
  68:      a K2 without them goes green exactly when a product fix has FAILED (measured, pass 1).
  69:   Negative controls (PRETABLE, not shipped): omit the `quit` -> the next `reg` succeeds and H2
  70:   reds; move the teardown ahead of the observation -> H2 must red (it does not observe anything).
  71: 
  72: ARM S (#545) -- the five declined sites in ONE PROCESS.  Each site is accessed TWICE in its
  73: undefined direction with a LEGAL read of a sibling register between the two, and every access
  74: must (i) leave the process alive, (ii) leave all five interrupt registers unchanged, and (iii)
  75: announce EXACTLY ONCE across the whole arm, in the site's own text.  ONE process is the whole
  76: point: a latch shared between two sites, or one aggregate latch, is INVISIBLE to any design
  77: that runs one site per process -- measured in pass 1, two total escapes of a per-site design.
  78:   enable 0x30 -> +0x188 (IRQ), 0xc0 -> +0x288 (FIQ); decline writes carry 0x10 (IRQ, inside
  79:   0x30) and 0x40 (FIQ, inside 0xc0), so a store into the status field would show in BOTH the
  80:   raw and the masked read.
  81:   N1 str 0x10 -> +0x180 x2   (legal read between: +0x184 -> r7, row S2)
  82:   N2 str 0x10 -> +0x184 x2   (legal read between: +0x180 -> sl, row S1)
  83:   N3 ldr +0x18c x2 -> r9, r8 (both 0: the IRQEnable value that used to be computed there
  84:                               is 188h's answer; legal read between: +0x188 -> fp, row S3 = 0x30)
  85:   N4 str 0x40 -> +0x280 x2   (legal read between: +0x280 -> r2, row S4)
  86:   N5 str 0x40 -> +0x284 x2   (legal read between: +0x284 -> lr, row S5)
  87:   SF1/SF2 FINAL raw reads +0x184 -> r1, +0x284 -> sp, after every decline (a store landed by
  88:      the LAST write of any pair shows here; r1 is A2's register in arm L and is free here).
  89:   N0 the five distinct `(once)` texts each appear exactly once in the arm's diagnostics
  90:      (5 on the fix; 4 under a two-site shared latch; 1 under one aggregate latch -- measured).
  91:   N1..N5 per-site: the site's own text appears exactly once (ACCESS-LOCAL text, so a misnamed
  92:      register reds its own row, not a total).
  93:   Widths (a byte or halfword decline) are NOT rowed here: a `len == 4` conjunct on the decline
  94:   cannot be seen by a count that another access of the same site already latched.  They are
  95:   rowed OFFLINE in regress/diff_footbridge.c (P-rows), which also pins state PRESERVATION with
  96:   a NONZERO seeded status -- something no stepped guest can reach here (Q2 f).
  97:   S3 (a legal EnableClear write works, a zero write preserves) is E5's row already.
  98: 
  99: ARM E -- #539's ACCEPT rows, in a FOURTH arm so that arms L/T/K are byte-identical to what
 100: they were.  E carries its own C1/A1 (E0 = 0 is also what an ABSENT device returns, so the
 101: E rows are meaningless unless this arm demonstrably reached the device) and then:
 102:   E0 read IRQEnable before any write            -> 0            (reset value, 7.3.31)
 103:   E1 write P=0x2002 to Set, read               -> P            (readable; catches a bit
 104:                                                                   swap, a constant, a wrong
 105:                                                                   field, a masked value)
 106:   E2 write Q=0x60000000 to Set, read           -> P|Q          (pins |=; bit 30 set so a
 107:                                                                   diagnostic kept for the top
 108:                                                                   bits fires here)
 109:   E3 ldrb  +0x188                              -> 0x02         (low byte: len 1 reaches the
 110:   E6 ldrh  +0x188                              -> 0x2002        same case; the CSR drives
 111:                                                                   D[31:0] and the SA-110 takes
 112:                                                                   the lane, DC21285 3.2.x)
 113:   E5 write P to Clear, read Set                -> Q            (Clear works; = the E2 value
 114:                                                                   with P removed)
 115:   E4 no BRACKETED diagnostic naming footbridge anywhere in arm E -- the `fatals` regex is
 116:      `[ ... footbridge ... ]`, the house diagnostic shape; a kept fatal() with its exit gone
 117:      is caught; a diagnostic rewritten WITHOUT brackets or without the device name is NOT
 118:      (listed under NOT COVERED below)
 119: Execution order is E0,E1,E2,E3,E6,E5: E6 wants 0x2002, which is true only while P is set.
 120: P and Q are loaded from RAM (DATA+8, DATA+12), like 0x12345678: P is not a rotated ARM
 121: immediate (bits 1 and 13); Q is (0x06 ROR 4) and is loaded the same way for uniformity.
 122: Values land in r7,r1,r2,r9,fp,ip; r1/r2 are A2's registers in arm L and are free here
 123: because arm E does not score A2.  TESTED PATTERNS, stated so the contract is checkable:
 124: P = bits 1,13 and Q = bits 29,30 only; bit 31 and every other bit are untested by value.
 125: 
 126: CONTRACT (what this detector is designed to catch, and what it is not).  On the SET read
 127: branch: the exit reinstated (E0 dies), reinstated only when irq_enable == 0 (E0), a wrong
 128: field/constant/mask/bit-swap (E1, E2), a width guard (E3, E6), a kept diagnostic (E4), a
 129: diagnostic kept for bit 30 (E4; E2 if it exits -- bit 31 is never set here, so a bit-31-only
 130: guard is UNTESTED).  On the CLEAR arms: a broken `&= ~`
 131: (E5).  On the FIVE DECLINED SITES (#545): the exit reinstated at any one site (that N row's
 132: arm dies: N0 and every S row red), the announce unlatched (N-row count 2), a shared or
 133: aggregate latch (N0 count 4 or 1), the +0x18c read leaking irq_enable (N3: 0x30 not 0), a
 134: decline storing into a status field (S1/S2/S4/S5/SF1/SF2), a misnamed register (its N row),
 135: a latch cleared by its own read (N3 count 2).  The harness legs H1/H2 as described above.
 136: NOT COVERED by any GUEST row here: (f) a legal status read narrowed to a CONSTANT 0 -- irq_status
 137: is 0 on every path a stepped guest can reach (the timer assert is host-timer driven and the
 138: host timers are stopped at the prompt; measured with timer 1 armed), so S1/S2/S4/S5/SF1/SF2 pin
 139: the VALUE 0 and catch a widening (`&` -> `|`, `odata = enable`), never a narrowing -- the offline
 140: P-rows in diff_footbridge.c seed a nonzero status and pin that; the FIQ read EXPRESSION is
 141: unpinned everywhere (`fiq_status` has no writer in the tree: `fiqenableread`).  +0x28c
 142: (FIQEnableClear read, the FIQ twin of +0x18c) already declined SILENTLY before #545 and is
 143: deliberately unchanged -- a read of it returns 0 before and after, so there is no witness;
 144: it is `fiqenableread` facet (2), where the Opus seat's dissent (fix it in the same commit)
 145: is recorded verbatim.  190h/290h (IRQSoft/FIQSoft) reach `default:` and announce on every
 146: access: `fbirqsoftunimpl`.  (a) no row observes the
 147: CPU IRQ line -- an edit to the write arm's assert condition (`irq_status | irq_enable`) is
 148: invisible to every row here, because cpu_dyntrans.c suppresses interrupt delivery under
 149: `step` and CPSR I/F stay set; it needs a free-running arm with a positive delivery control,
 150: filed with IRQSoft; (b) +0x189..+0x18b: real hardware selects the same CSR by A[10:2] and
 151: returns lanes 1-3, the model routes them to `default:` and returns 0 -- a device-wide decode
 152: deviation, filed, not rowed; (c) irq_status and fiq_enable are 0 throughout, so a mutant
 153: returning either at +0x188 is caught by E1/E2 only through the value, not distinguished
 154: from a constant 0.  (d) D1 compares the disassembly of every STEPPED word; the landing pad is
 155: planted and never stepped, and a step that ends the process is not compared.  (e) E4 sees only
 156: a BRACKETED diagnostic that names footbridge (the `fatals` regex); a bare-text diagnostic on
 157: the read path escapes it.
 158: TEARDOWN CONVENTION this file's bounded drain rests on: rd() returns True on a select TIMEOUT
 159: and False only on EOF/EIO, so the post-quit drain loop breaks on EOF or its deadline, never on
 160: a quiet interval (the same convention as the two pending probes' Session.rd).
 161: 
 162: EVERY PLANTED WORD IS CHECKED AGAINST THE DISASSEMBLY THE EMULATOR PRINTS AS IT STEPS IT --
 163: not against a separate `unassemble`, because the step line is the instruction that actually
 164: executed.  This project has a recorded incident where a wrong register field made a gate row
 165: measure the wrong thing for months.
 166: 
 167: COST, measured: see PROBE_WALL below -- 6 cold-debugger launches (#545: +S, +Q, +H), no boot.  A booting-rig row
 168: was deliberately NOT written: no rig in this tree boots a footbridge machine at all, which is
 169: the gap this probe closes, and adding one would put a load-sensitive wall-clock oracle into a
 170: battery already carrying ~37.
 171: 
 172: usage: footbridge_sites_probe.py <gxemul-binary> <four-byte-raw-stub>
 173: The stub is the same scaffolding gate_arm.sh writes for testarm: `cats` needs a file
 174: argument to construct, and every word of it is overwritten before anything executes.
 175: """
 176: import os, pty, re, select, signal, sys, time
