   2: """`fbpending`: a RUNG-3 cold-debugger witness that the emulated footbridge clock
   3: STOPS for 42.9 s in every 85.9 s, on an UNMODIFIED in-tree `-E cats`.
   4: 
   5: THE DEFECT (as it stood before its fix; the citations are to the pre-fix file and are kept
   6: as history).  dev_footbridge.c declared `int pending_timer_interrupts[4]`; the four timer
   7: callbacks did `->pending_timer_interrupts[N] ++` with no bound; and DEVICE_TICK(footbridge)
   8: delivered a tick ONLY under `if (... [i] > 0)`.
   9: Nothing decrements the counter except a guest write to TIMER_x_CLEAR, so a guest that
  10: programs a fast timer and does not acknowledge drives the counter to INT_MAX, where `++`
  11: is signed overflow (UB) and, in practice, wraps to INT_MIN.  For the next 2^31
  12: increments the `> 0` test is FALSE: no INTERRUPT_ASSERT, and timer_value stops being
  13: re-randomised.
  14: 
  15: THE RATE, arithmetic this probe measures rather than assumes.  The increments happen in
  16: the SIGALRM handler (core/timer.c:254-290), at up to TIMER_MAX_CATCHUP (1048576) per
  17: timer per signal, TIMER_BASE_FREQUENCY (65.0 Hz) signals per second -- a ceiling of
  18: 68,157,440 increments per WALL-CLOCK second.  The footbridge timer's own rate is
  19: machine->emulated_hz / effective_cycles, and cats sets emulated_hz = 50,000,000
  20: (machine_cats.c:56-57), so TIMER_1_LOAD = 1 asks for 50e6 Hz, under the ceiling and
  21: therefore delivered in full.  2^31 / 50e6 = 42.95 s to the wrap and 2^32 / 50e6 =
  22: 85.90 s to the return, giving the 42.9-in-85.9 duty cycle this probe plots.
  23: 
  24: THE GUEST-VISIBLE DISCRIMINATOR, and why it needs no interrupt plumbing.  TIMER_1_VALUE
  25: (dev_footbridge.c `case TIMER_1_VALUE`) is READ/WRITE: a read returns d->timer_value[0], a write
  26: stores `idata & TIMER_MAX_VAL`.  DEVICE_TICK overwrites that same field with
  27: `random() % footbridge_effective_cycles(load, control, 0)` -- and with load == 1 that
  28: modulus is 1, so EVERY delivered tick writes exactly 0.  So the guest:
  29: 
  30:     writes 0x00AB0000 into TIMER_1_VALUE, then polls it.
  31:     It reads back 0x00AB0000 ... until a tick lands, and then reads 0.
  32: 
  33: Each 0 is one delivered tick, counted by the guest into RAM.  Pre-wrap that count climbs
  34: at the device-tick rate; post-wrap it FREEZES and the sentinel the guest wrote survives
  35: indefinitely.  No interrupt controller, no IRQ enable, no guest OS, no disk image.
  36: (This used to add "an IRQ enable read would hit an unrelated fatal()+exit(1)"; #539
  37: removed that exit, and the enable registers play no part in this witness.)
  38: 
  39: *** WHY A FROZEN COUNTER IS NOT MERELY "THE EMULATOR STOPPED", which is the whole
  40:     difficulty of a negative measurement.  Three quantities run CONCURRENTLY in the same
  41:     guest loop and are published to separate RAM words every iteration: ***
  42: 
  43:       0x9024  iteration count   -- climbs whether or not the device does anything.  If
  44:                                    this freezes, the EMULATOR stopped and the whole
  45:                                    measurement is void.  It is a liveness control.
  46:       0x902c  VENDOR_ID re-read -- a fresh guest ldr from footbridge+0x00 on EVERY
  47:                                    iteration.  0x1011 here during the frozen window
  48:                                    proves the device is still MAPPED and still
  49:                                    DISPATCHING; what stopped is the TICK, not the
  50:                                    device.  RAM cannot produce 0x1011.
  51:       0x9020  delivered ticks   -- the thing under test.
  52: 
  53:     A freeze in 0x9020 while 0x9024 climbs and 0x902c still reads 0x1011 is a statement
  54:     about dev_footbridge.c and about nothing else.
  55: 
  56: THE MMU TRAP (documented by footbridge_sites_probe.py, whose arm T this reuses).
  57: machine_cats.c calls arm_setup_initial_translation_table(), which maps only the low
  58: 256 MB and IGNORES the top VA nibble.  The footbridge is at PA 0x42000000, outside that
  59: window, so under the boot MMU every device read returns 0 with a RAM liveness row still
  60: green.  Hence arm T: the same program with the three MMU-disable words replaced by nops
  61: MUST NOT produce the device's answers.
  62: 
  63: ARMS
  64:   A  the witness.  MMU off, TIMER_1_LOAD=1, TIMER_1_CONTROL=TIMER_ENABLE, then
  65:      free-running in wall-clock slices with the counters sampled out of RAM between
  66:      them.  Default 23 x 5 s = 115 s of free-run, which brackets BOTH the wrap at
  67:      42.95 s and the return at 85.90 s.
  68:   E  encodings.  Byte-identical to A except ONE immediate: the sentinel is 0 instead of
  69:      0x00AB0000, which makes the poll compare equal on the first pass and therefore
  70:      walks EVERY instruction of both loop bodies under `step 1`.  That is what lets the
  71:      hand-assembled words of the loop -- the words actually under test -- be checked
  72:      against the disassembly the emulator prints as it EXECUTES them, rather than
  73:      against a separate `unassemble` of words that might never run.
  74:   T  negative control.  MMU left on.  Must not return 0x1011 or 0x00345678.
  75:   K  failability control.  Arm A's prologue plus ONE instruction, a guest read of
  76:      IRQ_ENABLE_CLEAR (footbridge+0x18c, write-only per the DC21285), which
  77:      dev_footbridge.c's `case IRQ_ENABLE_CLEAR` read branch answers with fatal() then
  78:      exit(1).  A probe in which nothing can die proves nothing by surviving; K must
  79:      kill the host (its complaint AND exit 1) and A must not (exit 0).  #539 moved this
  80:      from +0x188, which is a documented read-only register and now answers reads; the
  81:      bridge is retired by the round that declines +0x18c.
  82: 
  83: CONTROLS ARE NOT OPTIONAL AND ABSENT DATA MUST FAIL.  Every RAM word is poisoned with
  84: 0xDEADBEEF before the run, every verdict row first requires the values it consults to be
  85: PRESENT, and an arm that did not reach SURVIVED voids every row that reads it.  This
  86: project has shipped a row that reported ok by comparing two -1 defaults.
  87: 
  88: PRE-FIX / POST-FIX POLARITY.  There is no fix at the time of writing, so what this file
  89: can show is the FIRST clause of witness validity only: the symptom on the committed
  90: build.  It is written so the SAME file is the post-fix detector -- FBPENDING_VERDICT=PASS
  91: means the controls held AND the run was LONG ENOUGH to see the window AND the clock never
  92: stopped.
  93: 
  94: *** A RUN THAT WAS TOO SHORT MUST NOT CERTIFY A FIX, which is "absent data must fail"
  95:     applied to the TIME axis and is a trap this probe walked into while being written.
  96:     At 11 x 5 s the wrap is reached at 43.1 s and only 10 s of dead window fits inside
  97:     the run, which is under the freeze threshold -- so the probe printed SYMPTOM=ABSENT,
  98:     VERDICT=PASS, exit 0, on a build with the defect fully present.  A shorter run than
  99:     that would not have reached the wrap at all and would have looked even cleaner.
 100:     Hence FBPENDING_COVERAGE: absence is only reportable once the free-run total has
 101:     passed the predicted RETURN at 85.90 s, and otherwise the symptom is UNDETERMINED
 102:     rather than ABSENT. ***
 103: 
 104: KNOWN BLIND SPOT, stated rather than discovered later.  This measures the guest-visible
 105: SYMPTOM, not the undefined behaviour.  A "fix" that merely made the counter unsigned
 106: would remove the freeze and pass every row here while leaving a wrap in place; only a
 107: sanitizer build (regress/build_asan.sh does pass -fsanitize=address,undefined) or a row
 108: that inspects the counter's own bound can speak to that.
 109: 
 110: usage: fbpending_witness.py <gxemul-binary> <four-byte-raw-stub>
 111:                             [--slices N] [--slice-seconds S] [--learn]
