  62:   H2 QUIT AFTER A VERIFIED PROLOGUE: arm L's program, stepped to its end, a FRESH `reg` proving
  63:      r3 bit 0 clear, r5 = 0x11223344, r6 = 0x1011 and r1 = 0x00345678 (the prologue really
  64:      ran), then `quit`, then ONE MORE ordinary `reg`: it must fail and `dead` must be set
  65:      BEFORE teardown, and the reap must be `WIFEXITED && == 0`.  Both non-obvious clauses are
  66:      load-bearing: a child that died BEFORE the prologue finished produces `next send fails`
  67:      and `dead` IDENTICALLY, and is separated only by exit == 0 and the fresh-reg values --
  68:      a K2 without them goes green exactly when a product fix has FAILED (measured, pass 1).
  69:   Negative controls (PRETABLE, not shipped): omit the `quit` -> the next `reg` succeeds and H2
  70:   reds (measured: M12).  NOT COVERED, and measured so (pass 2): moving the teardown AHEAD of the
  71:   H2 observation keeps H2 GREEN -- close() quits, drains and reaps exit 0, and the later `reg`
  72:   then fails on the closed descriptor with `dead` set, so the row cannot tell "death noticed
  73:   mid-session" from "descriptor already closed".  That reorder is a harness edit, not a
  74:   product one; the same reorder WITHOUT the quit is caught, but only by the exit == 0 clause
  75:   (the closed pty SIGHUPs the live child).
  76: 
  77: ARM S (#545) -- the five declined sites in ONE PROCESS.  Each site is accessed TWICE in its
  78: undefined direction with a LEGAL read of a sibling register between the two, and every access
  79: must (i) leave the process alive, (ii) leave all five interrupt registers unchanged, and (iii)
  80: announce EXACTLY ONCE across the whole arm, in the site's own text.  ONE process is the whole
  81: point: a latch shared between two sites, or one aggregate latch, is INVISIBLE to any design
  82: that runs one site per process -- measured in pass 1, two total escapes of a per-site design.
  83:   enable 0x30 -> +0x188 (IRQ), 0xc0 -> +0x288 (FIQ); decline writes carry 0x10 (IRQ, inside
  84:   0x30) and 0x40 (FIQ, inside 0xc0), so a store into the status field would show in BOTH the
  85:   raw and the masked read.
  86:   N1 str 0x10 -> +0x180 x2   (legal read between: +0x184 -> r7, row S2)
  87:   N2 str 0x10 -> +0x184 x2   (legal read between: +0x180 -> sl, row S1)
  88:   N3 ldr +0x18c x2 -> r9, r8 (both 0: the IRQEnable value that used to be computed there
  89:                               is 188h's answer; legal read between: +0x188 -> fp, row S3 = 0x30)
  90:   N4 str 0x40 -> +0x280 x2   (legal read between: +0x280 -> r2, row S4)
  91:   N5 str 0x40 -> +0x284 x2   (legal read between: +0x284 -> lr, row S5)
  92:   SF1/SF2 FINAL raw reads +0x184 -> r1, +0x284 -> sp, after every decline (a store landed by
  93:      the LAST write of any pair shows here; r1 is A2's register in arm L and is free here).
  94:   N0 the five distinct `(once)` texts each appear exactly once in the arm's diagnostics
  95:      (5 on the fix; 4 under a two-site shared latch; 1 under one aggregate latch -- measured).
  96:   N1..N5 per-site: the site's own text appears exactly once (ACCESS-LOCAL text, so a misnamed
  97:      register reds its own row, not a total).
  98:   Widths (a byte or halfword decline) are NOT rowed here: a `len == 4` conjunct on the decline
  99:   cannot be seen by a count that another access of the same site already latched.  They are
 100:   rowed OFFLINE in regress/diff_footbridge.c (P-rows), which also pins state PRESERVATION with
 101:   a NONZERO seeded status -- something no stepped guest can reach here (Q2 f).
 102:   S3 (a legal EnableClear write works, a zero write preserves) is E5's row already.
 103: 
 104: ARM E -- #539's ACCEPT rows, in a FOURTH arm so that arms L/T/K are byte-identical to what
 105: they were.  E carries its own C1/A1 (E0 = 0 is also what an ABSENT device returns, so the
 106: E rows are meaningless unless this arm demonstrably reached the device) and then:
 107:   E0 read IRQEnable before any write            -> 0            (reset value, 7.3.31)
 108:   E1 write P=0x2002 to Set, read               -> P            (readable; catches a bit
 109:                                                                   swap, a constant, a wrong
 110:                                                                   field, a masked value)
 111:   E2 write Q=0x60000000 to Set, read           -> P|Q          (pins |=; bit 30 set so a
 112:                                                                   diagnostic kept for the top
 113:                                                                   bits fires here)
 114:   E3 ldrb  +0x188                              -> 0x02         (low byte: len 1 reaches the
 115:   E6 ldrh  +0x188                              -> 0x2002        same case; the CSR drives
 116:                                                                   D[31:0] and the SA-110 takes
 117:                                                                   the lane, DC21285 3.2.x)
 118:   E5 write P to Clear, read Set                -> Q            (Clear works; = the E2 value
 119:                                                                   with P removed)
